As the number of particles 

\[ N \rightarrow \infty \quad \text{and the mass of each particle becomes infinitesimally small (approaching a continuous distribution)}
\] 


, the summation is replaced by an integral: 

\[ \mathbf{R} = \frac{1}{M_{total}} \int \mathbf{r} dm \quad \text{or} \quad \mathbf{R} = \frac{1}{M_{total}} \int \rho(\mathbf{r}) \mathbf{r} dV \quad \text{for continuous densities.}
\] 


If one particle has a mass 

\[ m_k \quad \text{much larger than all other particles combined}
\] 


, then the center of mass 

\[ \mathbf{R} \quad \text{will be very close to the position vector of that heavy particle } \mathbf{r}_k \quad \text{(i.e., } \mathbf{R} \approx \mathbf{r}_k\text{).}
\] 


If all particles are located at the same position vector 

\[ \mathbf{r}_i = \mathbf{r}_0 \quad \text{for all } i \quad \text{then } \mathbf{R} = \mathbf{r}_0 \quad \text{regardless of their individual masses.}
\] 


.
